{
  "title": "Masked figure comparisons separate the sunflower proposals for f33v and f93r",
  "statement": "On the sampled drawings f33v shares a bounded oval disk and projecting rays with the named sunflower prints. Both head readers distinguish their simple ray tips from the divided f33v tips, and the 1479 Calendula head matches f33v on all five coded features. Whole-plant readers agree on deeply divided f33v blades and rounded lower bodies, while both named sunflower prints show simple blades and lack a below-ground region. They leave f33v leaf insertion unknown. The separate f93r head is tapered, punctate and lacks a discrete ray fringe in these views. Its head scores are higher against the 1542 Petasites illustration than against either sunflower, although f93r has a closer whole-plant score with Dodoens. These comparisons do not establish either plant identity or a word-to-picture anchor.",
  "program": "L4",
  "check": "Recreate the separately coded grayscale head and whole-plant masks from the source images, using the coordinates below. Have two new readers for each region apply the fixed five-feature rubric, recording missing regions and prior exposure before unmasking. Compare each folio separately to both named sunflower prints and the Old World controls using agreement counts and comparable-feature denominators. Include a close root-detail recheck and leave any anatomical contact unresolved when stroke overlap hides it.",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": true,
  "question": "K-033-open-1; Stolfi 1998 open question about f33v. These are completed observations attached to the desk step.",
  "historical_claims": [
    {
      "source": "https://www.ic.unicamp.br/~stolfi/EXPORT/00-EXPORT/98-01-17-sunflower/",
      "note": "Stolfi discusses f33v, including disk, rays and possible bracts, and objections from leaf division, apparent insertion, divided ray tips and rounded underground forms."
    },
    {
      "source": "https://doi.org/10.2307/2856859",
      "page": 126,
      "note": "O’Neill 1944 says fol. 93, identified as Helianthus annuus and supported by six botanists, without listing diagnostic sunflower characters there."
    },
    {
      "source": "https://umb.herbalgram.org/media/3jojkmox/issue100.pdf",
      "printed_page": 79,
      "note": "Tucker and Talbert 2013 specify f93r for O’Neill’s sunflower, discuss compression of a tall plant, and propose Psacalium peltigerum separately for f33v. The latter identification is outside these coded comparisons."
    },
    {
      "source": "https://www.notulaebotanicae.ro/index.php/nbha/article/download/12015/8986/48289",
      "page": 1125,
      "note": "Janick 2020 records that the surviving Hernández 1651 sunflower plates use substituted European images, one reversed from the 1633 Gerard edition. We do not count that illustration as an independent comparator."
    }
  ],
