  "method": {
    "head_sampling": "Top-left f33v head used as primary; upper-right scored separately as a crop sensitivity, sharing one page and artist. f93r is a distinct target. Dodoens single head and Besler largest central open head. Old World controls chosen for disk, compact head or ambiguous attachment; credits below. The right scabious crop and the water lily crop omit part of the ray perimeter. The Dodoens crop cuts some peripheral tip strokes. Retain uncertainty codes and inspect original when coverage matters.",
    "whole_sampling": "Show full plant and the root region when present. Nymphaea shows only two separate leaf/flower shapes in the mask and cyclamen is a deliberately restricted root-only comparison. The latter cannot enter whole-plant rankings. The root systems of Dodoens and Besler, and one concealed f33v attachment, cannot be compared.",
    "rubrics": {
      "heads": {
        "distinct disk": [
          "distinct",
          "confluent"
        ],
        "ray length": [
          "absent",
          "short",
          "long"
        ],
        "ray tip": [
          "simple",
          "split_notched"
        ],
        "disk or core texture": [
          "smooth",
          "fine_dots",
          "coarse_cells",
          "other"
        ],
        "outline": [
          "flat_round_oval",
          "tapered_pyramidal",
          "other"
        ]
      },
      "whole": {
        "architecture": [
          "single_axis",
          "branched",
          "basal_cluster",
          "other"
        ],
        "developed head count": [
          "one",
          "multiple"
        ],
        "blade division and breadth": [
          "simple_broad",
          "simple_narrow",
          "deep_radial",
          "other_lobed"
        ],
        "blade attachment": [
          "edge",
          "interior",
          "sessile",
          "mixed"
        ],
        "root structure": [
          "fine_branching",
          "rounded_enlargement",
          "thick_axis",
          "other"
        ]
      }
    },
    "coding": "A ray is long at or above half the local disk radius, short below, absent if no distinct rays, and U if the relation is not clear. Margin or open basal notch is edge and attachment directly to a stem is sessile. Multiple means two or more visibly developed heads. All categories describe drawn structures. U means obscure, inapplicable, or not shown, with reasons below.",
    "scores": "All matches use number of equal non-U categories followed by number of jointly visible categories, separately for each reader, no weighting. At least three comparisons are required for ranking; tables also retain the smaller denominators. Readers were given rubric and independent codes before seeing the source map. Sample is purposive; ranks are descriptive.",
    "prior_exposure": [
      "The organizer knew all sources and the original arguments before mask preparation and did not enter the independent ratings.",
      "Four model reader instances scored separate panels, two per region. All report no previous viewing they could recall of the exact masked images or each other’s ratings. They received anonymous IDs and no identity key or literature.",
      "The sunflower topic was inferable from the task or folder. Some readers recognized engraving, manuscript writing and sunflower-like silhouettes, and one head reader noticed the likely repeat. This was label masking with independent scoring, with incomplete provenance blinding.",
      "The readers are model instances in one agent session, with shared model family and some general manuscript context. Four votes are not four independent botanical assessments."
    ]
  },
