    "interpretation": "Dropping disk boundary, outer ray length and silhouette retains two more specific head features. Both f33v views remain closer to the sampled Calendula than to either sunflower in this subset. Crosshatched textures can arise from different drawing conventions, so this is not a biological identification."
  },
  "qualitative_checks": [
    {
      "feature": "f33v blade attachment",
      "observation": "Pale interior marks and inward-running veins are visible in several blades. Neither whole reader could trace a clear petiole-to-blade junction through the opaque fill. Nymphaea offers a visible marginal notch comparator; Petasites attachment was disputed between readers."
    },
    {
      "feature": "f33v roots",
      "observation": "Both readers marked round enlargements attached to branches on f33v. Both coded the cropped cyclamen root U because the organ context was insufficient, and neither historical sunflower prints the root. The c. 1280–1350 Egerton MS 747 f18v (https://bl.digirati.io/images/ark:/81055/vdc_100058663074.0x00002c/canvas/c/44) also offers a round body with radiating fine strokes, but the two herbal cyclamen images may be descended from a related illustrated tradition. Root specificity remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "feature": "f33v habit in independent print",
      "observation": "Besler shows several heads with side branches and exposed pointed structures, so those characters can occur in historical sunflower illustrations. Whole readers split on whether its main stem dominates."
    },
    {
      "feature": "f93r independent proposal",
      "observation": "The pointed, continuous head has fine dots. Both head readers recorded absent outer rays and unknown ray-tip shape. Whole readers agree on one dominant upright and one head, direct-looking blade contacts, and fine root strands. They disagree on broad/narrow. There is no combined f33v/f93r score."
    },
    {
      "feature": "sample limits",
      "observation": "No taxon can be rejected reliably from this small purposive image sample. Historical images can inherit other images and visual features are correlated. Head masks and whole drawings answer different questions and cannot be combined as independent evidence. There is no held-out score and no text pairing."
    },
    {
      "feature": "Peltation visibility control outside rankings",
      "source": "https://archive.org/details/PetriAndreaMatt00Matt/page/n1293/mode/1up",
      "crop": [
        749,
        272,
        2211,
        2739
      ],
      "description": "Mattioli 1565 p.1122, named Umbilicus Veneris. The organizer observed clear interior blade dimples and underside views on the Old World navelwort plate. The two whole readers kept the corresponding f33v contact unknown. This is a supplementary unmasked illustration check without points."
    }
  ],
